From 1fb4d68a59a4c7a05d4ff75ad1fbe0aa6e49d317 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 03:00:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 04/10] feat(api): add PUT and PATCH for inference services

A deployed service could not be changed through the API at all: the
only way to alter its desired state was to delete and re-create it.

PUT /api/v1/inference-services/{name} replaces spec, labels and
annotations; PATCH on the same path applies an RFC 7386 JSON merge
patch (application/merge-patch+json, otherwise 415) to the current
representation and then follows the same rules. Both need the write
scope and take the namespace query parameter like GET and DELETE.
The update goes through SQLiteRegistry.update_spec, so only a spec
change increments generation, a no-op update is not written, and the
status written by the controller is never overwritten.

kind, apiVersion, metadata.name, metadata.namespace and the tenant are
immutable (400 when a document tries to change them); status,
conditions, finalizers, generation and observedGeneration are ignored
on input. metadata.uid, metadata.resourceVersion and If-Match are
optimistic concurrency preconditions: a mismatch, as well as an update
of a Terminating resource, is a 409 error envelope whose
details.reason says why. A failed If-Match also returns 409 rather
than 412, so clients handle every concurrency failure the same way.

Single-resource responses (GET, POST, PUT, PATCH) carry a strong ETag
built from the uid and the resource version, so a resource deleted
and re-created under the same name, whose version restarts at 0,
never matches an old ETag. Without client preconditions the server
retries a few times when the controller writes in between read and
write.

Create now rejects unknown top-level fields with 400 instead of
silently ignoring them, so the same document is accepted or rejected
the same way by POST and PUT, and a GET response can be posted back.
